WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:08.441
This is DG32-LITE as recorded in its tape-in block diagram: the die as built, block by block.

2
00:00:08.991 --> 00:00:21.726
It covers the clock and reset, the lockstep pair, memory and fetch, the bus and the eighteen blocks around it, the pad plan, production test, and the four sign-off gates the die must pass.

3
00:00:22.276 --> 00:00:26.043
It is a design record, not a measurement of silicon.

4
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.364
The short version.

5
00:00:28.914 --> 00:00:36.819
One fifty megahertz clock domain, with the lockstep core measured at fifty-five to sixty-two megahertz after place-and-route.

6
00:00:37.369 --> 00:00:42.744
A checker that runs two cycles behind and sees the main core's bus responses.

7
00:00:43.294 --> 00:00:46.591
Forty-four wrapper pads, every one of them allocated.

8
00:00:47.141 --> 00:00:51.104
And four sign-off gates that must all pass on the final die.

9
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.473
One clock and one reset drive the whole block.

10
00:00:56.023 --> 00:00:59.048
The clock arrives from a pad at fifty megahertz.

11
00:00:59.598 --> 00:01:08.862
Eight software clock gates switch off idle peripherals: both UARTs, SPI, I squared C, PWM, the timers, the DMA and the CORDIC.

12
00:01:09.412 --> 00:01:12.014
Test mode forces every gate open.

13
00:01:12.564 --> 00:01:20.993
Reset is asynchronous, the supervisor deglitches the supply-good inputs, and the checker leaves reset two cycles after the main core.

14
00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:25.616
The lockstep pair is built to survive real firmware.

15
00:01:26.166 --> 00:01:29.201
The main core runs the code and owns the bus.

16
00:01:29.751 --> 00:01:38.748
The checker replays the same work two cycles later, with its inputs and bus responses mirrored, and the comparator checks every committed store.

17
00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:47.037
So interrupts are safe, firmware may branch on a peripheral read, and the pair has been verified under interrupts in simulation.

18
00:01:47.587 --> 00:01:49.840
A mismatch drives the fault pad.

19
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.086
Code runs from SRAM through a dedicated fetch port.

20
00:01:55.636 --> 00:02:03.010
The fetch decoder sends boot fetches to the ROM and application fetches to the SRAM's second port.

21
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:07.020
Anything else is a bus error and an instruction fault.

22
00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:16.964
The ROM is sixteen thousand words of mask-programmed logic that prints the banner, checks the flash header, copies the application and jumps.

23
00:02:17.514 --> 00:02:20.178
Blank flash opens a UART monitor.

24
00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:24.057
The bus serves one requester at a time.

25
00:02:24.607 --> 00:02:33.604
The CPU has priority over the DMA, and each read or write target is latched at the request and held until the response.

26
00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:37.484
Every peripheral is a thirty-two-bit register slave.

27
00:02:38.034 --> 00:02:46.428
A clock-gated or undecoded slot answers with an error instead of hanging, and the QSPI window serves data, not code.

28
00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:49.889
Eighteen blocks sit around that bus.

29
00:02:50.439 --> 00:02:57.519
Six handle connectivity: the UARTs, SPI, I squared C, the QSPI window, GPIO and the timers.

30
00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:04.610
Six serve the motor: PWM, DShot, encoder and Hall, CORDIC, the ADC and interrupts.

31
00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:14.210
And six handle system, safety and test: system control, the watchdog, the fault register, the error slave, scan, and the JTAG port.

32
00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:19.286
Every one of the forty-four wrapper pads has a job.

33
00:03:19.836 --> 00:03:28.316
Seven carry the QSPI flash, seven the PWM outputs and trigger, and six the encoder and Hall inputs.

34
00:03:28.866 --> 00:03:32.507
Four each go to the UARTs, SPI and JTAG.

35
00:03:33.057 --> 00:03:34.816
Three are GPIO.

36
00:03:35.366 --> 00:03:44.599
Two each carry I squared C, supply-good and the analog ADC inputs, and one each carry the clock, the reset and the fault output.

37
00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.230
Production test reaches the logic through JTAG.

38
00:03:49.780 --> 00:04:02.829
A tester drives the four JTAG pins into a test access port in the wrapper, which selects scan enable, scan mode, a chain, or bypass, and reaches thirteen scan chains inside the hardened block.

39
00:04:03.379 --> 00:04:08.116
Test mode forces every clock gate open, so scan reaches every block.

40
00:04:08.666 --> 00:04:13.268
Interactive CPU debug over JTAG is a roadmap item.

41
00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.213
Four gates must all pass before tape-in.

42
00:04:17.763 --> 00:04:20.145
The layout must be design-rule clean.

43
00:04:20.695 --> 00:04:24.627
Layout versus schematic must match uniquely, pins included.

44
00:04:25.177 --> 00:04:31.723
A connectivity check on the final layout must show every pad reaching logic, with no floating net.

45
00:04:32.273 --> 00:04:37.208
And a gate-level simulation of the routed netlist must boot and print the banner.

46
00:04:37.758 --> 00:04:43.036
The connectivity gate exists because the first two can pass while a pad pin floats.

47
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.092
Three things to take away.

48
00:04:46.642 --> 00:04:52.058
One clock and one reset, with eight clock gates and a checker released two cycles late.

49
00:04:52.608 --> 00:04:59.347
Nothing hangs and nothing floats: bad accesses return errors, and all forty-four pads are allocated.

50
00:04:59.897 --> 00:05:03.124
And four gates stand between the design and tape-in.

51
00:05:03.674 --> 00:05:08.474
The next milestone is first silicon, on the September twenty twenty-six shuttle.

